course: dictionary
lesson: 5491
title: Laughing: with, at, or a bird?
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one is meant. Laughing can mean making happy sounds because something is funny. It can also be a bird's call: «The laughing kookaburra is a big kingfisher whose family groups laugh together at dawn and dusk.»
explain: Look at the words next to laughing. «Laughing with friends» means sharing the fun together, so it is kind. «Laughing at someone's mistake» means making fun of them unkindly, so it is not kind. Ask yourself: is the laugh shared, or is it aimed at someone?
explain: Phrases need whole-group thinking. «Burst out laughing» means laughing suddenly. «Laughing your head off» means laughing very hard. «No laughing matter» means something serious, not a joke. Do not take a phrase word by word.
fact: Laughing means making happy sounds with your voice and face when something is funny.
fact: Laughing at someone means making fun of them unkindly.
fact: Laughing with someone means sharing the fun together.
fact: «Till he fell down flat on the bank» shows Tegumai laughing so hard that he fell over.
fact: A fit here means a sudden attack of laughing, as in «laugh till they nearly had two fits».
exercise: «We were laughing with our friends about the joke.» What does laughing with mean here? | Making fun of someone unkindly | Crying loudly | *Sharing the fun together | Running away from the group
exercise: «The kookaburra laughs together at dawn and dusk to mark its home.» What is the laughing in this sentence? | *A bird's call that marks its territory | A joke told by a bird | Laughing at a snake | A sad cry
exercise: «Tegumai laughed till he fell down flat on the bank.» What does this show? | *He laughed so hard he fell over | He was too tired to stand | He was angry and stamped his feet | He tripped by accident and did not laugh
test: The stoats laughed «till they nearly had two fits». What is a fit here? | A kind of hat | *A sudden attack of laughing | A small quarrel | A mark on the skin
test: Ella says «He is laughing at my mistake.» Is this kind? | It is a bird call | It means he is sad | *No, it makes fun of her unkindly | Yes, it shares the fun together
exam: Which sentence uses laughing to mean making fun of someone unkindly? | «The kookaburra laughs at dawn.» | «Laughter filled the room.» | «We were laughing with our friends.» | *«They were laughing at Jo's mistake.»
